<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Hume is a 64-year-old man with stage IIIB sigmoid colon cancer, resected in June, who is on day 9 of his third 21-day cycle of adjuvant CAPOX (oxaliplatin given on day 1, capecitabine on days 1 to 14). Three days ago he developed a runny nose and scratchy throat, followed by a dry cough. He called the oncology triage line this morning, as his chemotherapy plan instructs, and was asked to be seen in primary care today with a blood count. His home temperatures have been 37.1 to 37.4 °C, checked twice daily, and he has not taken acetaminophen, so no fever has been masked. A home COVID-19 antigen test yesterday was negative. He is eating and drinking normally, has had two soft stools a day without diarrhea, no mouth sores, and no redness or peeling of his palms or soles. The cold tingling in his fingers after each oxaliplatin dose settles within five days, as before. A friend who visited last week had a cold. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Three days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Nose, throat, and chest</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Three days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Clear nasal discharge, scratchy throat, dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Mild; sleeping normally</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Day 9 of the third CAPOX cycle</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Exposure to a visitor with a cold; on capecitabine</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Warm drinks help; no medicines taken</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No fever, chills, breathlessness, chest pain, diarrhea, mouth sores, or hand-foot symptoms</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
