    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection, without fever or neutropenia (J06.9)</content>. Afebrile at 37.3 °C with a clear chest and normal oxygen saturation. White cell count 4.6 and absolute neutrophil count 2.4 exclude neutropenia, so this is not febrile neutropenia and antibiotics are not indicated. [S]<list><item>Supportive care: fluids, rest, saline nasal rinse, and honey for the cough.</item><item>Check his temperature before any acetaminophen dose so that a fever is not masked.</item><item>Repeat the home COVID-19 antigen test in 48 hours; call the same day if it is positive, because antiviral treatment would be considered after a drug-interaction check.</item><item>Call the oncology 24-hour line at once, and go to the emergency department if told to, for a temperature of 38.0 °C or higher, chills, or breathlessness.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Stage IIIB sigmoid colon cancer on adjuvant CAPOX, cycle 3 (C18.7)</content>. No chemotherapy toxicity needing a dose change: no diarrhea, mucositis, or hand-foot syndrome, and the blood count allows treatment. [S]<list><item>Continue capecitabine to day 14 as prescribed; results and plan sent to the oncology team today, who agreed.</item><item>Stop capecitabine and call oncology if he develops four or more extra stools a day, mouth sores that stop him eating, or painful red palms or soles.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Colon Cancer, Version 4.2025 (2025); Outpatient Management of Fever and Neutropenia in Adults Treated for Malignancy: American Society of Clinical Oncology and Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guideline Update (2018); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Oncology team updated today. Nurse telephone check in two days. Cycle 4 as scheduled if well. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-19: review two weeks after completing adjuvant CAPOX and start of survivorship surveillance. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>
